<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ringstrasse</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ringstrasse is a grand circular boulevard in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> built in the second half of the nineteenth century on the site of the demolished city walls. It was lined with major public buildings, including parliament, the opera, museums and the university, as well as private residences. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a> cite the Ringstrasse development as an expression of the economic vitality behind Vienna's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fin-de-siecle">fin-de-siècle</a> cultural flowering.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ringstrasse.jpg" alt="Ringstrasse" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gugerell · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wien_01_Burgring_b.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The boulevard replaced the city walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, which had been built in the 13th century and first funded by the ransom paid for the release of Richard the Lionheart, captured near Vienna on his return from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-crusade">Third Crusade</a>. They were reinforced after the Ottoman Siege of Vienna in 1529 and the Thirty Years' War, and were surrounded by a glacis about 500 metres wide where building and planting were forbidden. By the late 18th century the fortifications were obsolete, and under Emperor Joseph II streets and walkways were laid out in the glacis.</p><p>In 1850 the suburbs were incorporated into the city, and the walls became an obstacle to traffic. In 1857 Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph I</a> issued the decree Es ist Mein Wille, ordering the demolition of the walls and moats and setting out the dimensions of the new boulevard and the locations and functions of the planned buildings.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Ringstrasse is a circular boulevard of 5.3 km running clockwise from the Urania to the Schottenring, in nine sections, most named after a prominent structure. Aristocrats and wealthy individuals hurried to build Ring Road palaces in the historicist style. Buildings along it include the Vienna State Opera, the Hofburg, the Kunsthistorisches Museum, the Naturhistorisches Museum, the Austrian Parliament Building, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgtheater">Burgtheater</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-vienna">University of Vienna</a> and the Votivkirche. Because the Ring was designed for appearance, a parallel street for freight, the Lastenstraße, later called Zweierlinie, was built beyond it.</p><p>Work was not finished until 1913 with the Ministry of War, when the historicist style was already unfashionable, as the Art Nouveau Postal Savings Bank by Otto Wagner, built opposite in 1906, shows. The Ringtheater fire of 1881 killed several hundred people.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Ring and its buildings were meant to display the grandeur and glory of the Habsburg Empire. On a practical level, the project was influenced by Emperor Napoléon III's planning in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, where wider streets had proved effective in preventing revolutionary barricades and in allowing artillery control. The Ringstrasse is designated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> as part of the Historic Centre of Vienna World Heritage Site. It remains a major route through the city's road network, with trams, two bike lanes, shops and hotels, and a route that tourists walk to see its sights.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ringstrasse” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Vienna, with several hundred thousand inhabitants, was the political and cultural centre, and in 1857 Franz Joseph ordered its old fortifications demolished to make way for the <b>Ringstrasse</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Vienna's fin-de-siècle culture rested on this prosperity: the <b>Ringstrasse</b>, the Secession movement, coffee-house intellectual life and the professional class that produced Sigmund Freud, Gustav Mahler, Gustav Klimt and Theodor Herzl all belonged to a prosperous, cosmopolitan urban economy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Karl Benz made the first public drive of his three-wheeled Patent-Motorwagen on the <b>Ringstrasse</b> in Mannheim.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-3">3 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
